      "id": 1,
      "text": "National City was founded in 1868 when Frank Kimball and brothers bought Rancho de la Nación.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitcalifornia.com/places-to-visit/national-city/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name comes from the Mexican ranch name Rancho de la Nación, meaning “Ranch of the Nation.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_City%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1885 the Santa Fe Railroad made National City its western terminus, and the 1882 depot still stands as a lone survivor of the original five transcontinental stations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        9,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_City%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Kile Morgan Local History Room in the National City Public Library holds original archives, maps, and yearbooks that document the city’s early orchards and Victorian homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_City%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "National City’s population of about 61,000 reflects a renaissance of growth and commerce, turning the once rural ranch into a diverse community.",
